NNAAMMEE
     vvmmccttll — control the virtual machine daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     vvmmccttll [--vv] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The vvmmccttll utility is used to control the virtual machine monitor (VMM)
     subsystem.  A VMM manages virtual machines (VMs) on a host.  The VMM sub‐
     system is responsible for creating, destroying, and executing VMs.

     The --vv option enables verbose mode.  Within the commands, the _s_i_z_e argu‐
     ment can be specified with a human-readable scale, using the format de‐
     scribed in scan_scaled(3).  The _i_d argument can be either a numeric, non-
     zero identifier or alternatively the name of a virtual machine.

     The _n_a_m_e argument can only consist of alphanumeric characters, as well as
     '.', '-', and '_', and must start with a letter.

     The _d_i_s_k argument is used by commands that take a path to a disk image
     file.  It may be prefixed with a format prefix (raw:_d_i_s_k or qcow2:_d_i_s_k)
     in order to specify the disk image format.  If left unspecified, the for‐
     mat defaults to ‘raw’ if it cannot be derived automatically.

     The commands are as follows:

     ccoonnssoollee _i_d
             Using cu(1) connect to the console of the VM with the specified
             _i_d.

     ccrreeaattee [--bb _b_a_s_e | --ii _d_i_s_k] [--ss _s_i_z_e] _d_i_s_k
             Create a VM disk image file with the specified _d_i_s_k path.

             --bb _b_a_s_e   For ‘qcow2’, a _b_a_s_e image may be specified.  The base
                       image is not modified and the derived image contains
                       only the changes written by the VM.

             --ii _d_i_s_k   Copy and convert the input _d_i_s_k to the newly created
                       disk.  This option conflicts with --bb _b_a_s_e.

             --ss _s_i_z_e   Specify the _s_i_z_e of the new disk image, rounded to
                       megabytes.  If the --bb option is specified, the size
                       must match the size of the _b_a_s_e image.  For the --ii op‐
                       tion, the size cannot be smaller than the input disk
                       size.  The size can be omitted with the --bb and --ii op‐
                       tions and will be obtained from the base or input image
                       respectively.

     llooaadd _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Load additional configuration from the specified file.

     lloogg bbrriieeff | vveerrbboossee
             Disable or enable verbose debug logging.

     ppaauussee _i_d
             Pause a VM with the specified _i_d.

     rreellooaadd  Remove all stopped VMs and reload the configuration from the de‐
             fault configuration file.  VMs that are currently running will
             not have their configuration reloaded.  To reload configurations
             for currently running VMs, stop those VMs before issuing the
             reload command.

     rreesseett [aallll | sswwiittcchheess | vvmmss]
             Reset the running state, reset sswwiittcchheess, or reset and terminate
             all vvmmss.

     sshhooww [--rr] [_i_d]
             An alias for the ssttaattuuss command.

     ssttaarrtt [--ccLL] [--BB _d_e_v_i_c_e] [--bb _p_a_t_h] [--dd _d_i_s_k] [--ii _c_o_u_n_t] [--mm _s_i_z_e]
             [--nn _s_w_i_t_c_h] [--rr _p_a_t_h] [--tt _n_a_m_e] _i_d | _n_a_m_e
             Start a new VM _n_a_m_e with the specified parameters.  An existing
             VM may be started by referencing its _i_d.

             --BB _d_e_v_i_c_e  Force system to boot from the specified device for
                        this boot.  _d_e_v_i_c_e can be set to:

                        _c_d_r_o_m  Boot the CD-ROM image.
                        _d_i_s_k   Boot from disk.
                        _n_e_t    Perform a PXE boot using the first network in‐
                               terface.

                        Currently _n_e_t is only supported when booting a kernel
                        using the --bb flag while _d_i_s_k and _c_d_r_o_m only work with
                        VMs booted using BIOS.

             --bb _p_a_t_h    Boot the VM with the specified OpenBSD kernel or cus‐
                        tom BIOS image.  If not specified, the default is to
                        boot using the BIOS image in _/_e_t_c_/_f_i_r_m_w_a_r_e_/_v_m_m_-_b_i_o_s.
                        If the VM is an existing VM, use the provided image
                        for only the next boot.

             --cc         Automatically connect to the VM console.

             --dd _d_i_s_k    Use a disk image at the specified _d_i_s_k path (may be
                        specified multiple times to add multiple disk images).

             --ii _c_o_u_n_t   Number of network interfaces to add to the VM.

             --LL         Add a local network interface.  vmd(8) will auto-gen‐
                        erate an IPv4 subnet for the interface, configure a
                        gateway address on the VM host side, and run a simple
                        DHCP/BOOTP server for the VM.  See _L_O_C_A_L _I_N_T_E_R_F_A_C_E_S
                        below for more information on how addresses are calcu‐
                        lated and assigned when using the --LL option.

             --mm _s_i_z_e    Memory _s_i_z_e of the VM, rounded to megabytes.  The de‐
                        fault is 512M.  The maximum amount of memory assigna‐
                        ble to a VM is governed by the datasize parameter for
                        the vmd user in _/_e_t_c_/_l_o_g_i_n_._c_o_n_f.

             --nn _s_w_i_t_c_h  Add a network interface that is attached to the speci‐
                        fied virtual _s_w_i_t_c_h.  See the SWITCH CONFIGURATION
